// The contributor colour palette — `F-ID-4`, `AC-MAT-4`.
//
// Read by `treepo-id::palette`. A contributor's colour is a function of their `AuthorKey`
// and this file: the key picks an entry, then a point inside that entry's neighbourhood.
// No name, no email, and nothing about the repository enters it (`N9`, `N3`).
//
// # Units: OKLCh, integers, never floats
//
// `N3` keeps floats out of anything reaching generated output, so every number is an integer
// in a stated unit — the convention `lsystem.ron` and `folder-signals.ron` already use.
//
// * **lightness** — per mille of OKLab L. `800` is `0.80`. `0` is black, `1000` is white.
// * **chroma** — per mille of OKLab C, distance from grey. `110` is `0.11`. Roughly: `0` is
//   grey, `60` is muted, `150` is vivid, and past `250` nothing on a monitor can show it.
// * **hue** — millidegrees around the OKLCh circle. `140000` is 140°.
//
// The palette is authored in OKLCh rather than in hex because `AC-MAT-4` is a *perceptual*
// claim and hex is not a perceptual space. `treepo-id::palette` explains the choice at
// length; the short version is that LCh → Lab is one sine and one cosine, which
// `treepo-det::trig` computes identically on every platform, where sRGB → Lab needs a power
// and a cube root that no integer implementation here has. Converting to sRGB is the render
// layer's job, in Phase 5, downstream of everything `AC-DET-2` covers.
//
// **Gamut is not checked.** A high lightness with a high chroma is a valid OKLCh coordinate
// and not a colour a display can produce. The bands below stay conservative enough not to
// need mapping; if a later edit pushes chroma up, the render layer is where that gets
// resolved.
//
// # The shape of the palette
//
// Three lightness bands of six hues each. The bands are what make the palette read as a set
// rather than as a rainbow, and they are also most of the separation budget: 150 per mille of
// lightness between adjacent bands is on its own more than twice the threshold, so every
// cross-band pair is comfortable and only the within-band spacing is doing real work.
//
// Hues are 60° apart within a band and offset 20° between bands, so no two entries anywhere
// in the palette share a hue. Chroma rises in the middle band, because a mid lightness is
// where a display has the most room for it and where a muted entry would look washed out
// beside its neighbours.
//
// The hue names in the comments are approximate and are a reading aid. OKLCh hue angles do
// not line up with the HSL ones anybody has memorised.
//
// # `min_separation` and `jitter` are one decision, not two
//
// `jitter` turns each entry from a colour into a *family*: a contributor's key picks the
// family and then a point within this much of its centre, so a repository with more
// contributors than the palette has entries still shows them apart. What keeps `AC-MAT-4`
// true is that the loader subtracts **both** neighbourhood radii from every pairwise
// distance before comparing it to `min_separation`. Widening the jitter therefore tightens
// the palette, and widening it far enough fails the file rather than quietly eroding the
// guarantee.
//
// At the values below the tightest pair in the palette is 110 apart and needs 95, so there
// is about 15 per mille of headroom. That is the number to watch when editing.
(
    version: 1,

    // Per mille of OKLab distance. Around 20 is a just-noticeable difference on a large
    // field; a mosaic cell is small and is seen against wood rather than against white, so
    // this is set at three times that. It is a legibility judgement about a rendering that
    // does not exist yet, and it is in the file because Phase 5 is where it gets looked at.
    min_separation: 60,

    // Deliberately small. The families are the design; this is the variation inside one, and
    // it has to stay well under the gaps between them.
    jitter: (
        lightness: 10,   // per mille
        chroma: 8,       // per mille
        hue: 3000,       // millidegrees — 3°
    ),

    entries: [
        // Light band — L 0.80, chroma 0.11.
        (lightness: 800, chroma: 110, hue:  20000),  // rose
        (lightness: 800, chroma: 110, hue:  80000),  // amber
        (lightness: 800, chroma: 110, hue: 140000),  // green
        (lightness: 800, chroma: 110, hue: 200000),  // teal
        (lightness: 800, chroma: 110, hue: 260000),  // blue
        (lightness: 800, chroma: 110, hue: 320000),  // magenta

        // Mid band — L 0.65, chroma 0.14. The most saturated of the three.
        (lightness: 650, chroma: 140, hue:  40000),  // vermilion
        (lightness: 650, chroma: 140, hue: 100000),  // gold
        (lightness: 650, chroma: 140, hue: 160000),  // moss
        (lightness: 650, chroma: 140, hue: 220000),  // cyan
        (lightness: 650, chroma: 140, hue: 280000),  // indigo
        (lightness: 650, chroma: 140, hue: 340000),  // pink

        // Dark band — L 0.50, chroma 0.12.
        (lightness: 500, chroma: 120, hue:      0),  // red
        (lightness: 500, chroma: 120, hue:  60000),  // rust
        (lightness: 500, chroma: 120, hue: 120000),  // olive
        (lightness: 500, chroma: 120, hue: 180000),  // pine
        (lightness: 500, chroma: 120, hue: 240000),  // slate blue
        (lightness: 500, chroma: 120, hue: 300000),  // violet
    ],
)
